---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import DiversificationModule from "../../components/DiversificationModule.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Diversification"
  description="Diversification from first principles: how out-of-phase ups and downs cancel to lower risk. Start with the pure idea as interfering waves, then add real randomness and watch the free lunch shrink but not vanish."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Diversification: the only free lunch</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Mixing assets that don't move in lockstep lowers a portfolio's risk without
      lowering its expected return: the closest thing investing has to a free
      lunch. Meet it in two views: first the pure idea, where out-of-phase waves
      cancel; then the messy reality, where randomness means cancellation is only
      partial and every so often everything falls together.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <DiversificationModule client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Waves: the pure idea</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Each asset is a wave.</strong> Taller = more volatile; its
          <strong>correlation</strong> sets its phase. At +1 the waves line up; at
          −1 they're mirror images.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The portfolio is their sum.</strong> The dashed lines show how
          big the swing would be if risk simply stacked; the gap down to the solid
          wave is the risk that cancelled away. Slide correlation toward −1 and the
          portfolio flattens toward a straight line: near-perfect cancellation.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Randomness: the reality</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Nothing cancels perfectly.</strong> With real, noisy returns the
          theoretical benefit and what a given run actually delivers rarely match,
          and there's no truly risk-free blend of risky assets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Assets sink together.</strong> The higher the correlation, the
          more often every holding is down at once: exactly when diversification
          helps least. That's why correlations, not just volatilities, drive
          portfolio risk.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both views are simplified. Real returns have fat tails and history the
        model can't capture. To bring it together with the efficient frontier and
        a full range of outcomes, see
        <a href="/tools/portfolio">Portfolio, Allocation &amp; Bonds</a>.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="diversification" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/diversification" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
